# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: libredwg-msys2-compiler-path-lacks-exe — a cold libredwg configure cannot start under MSYS2, and the GitLab runner hides it
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-libredwg-msys2-exe

* Symptom

On a *cold* build directory under MSYS2, =make build-libredwg= dies in
=cmake= configuration:

#+begin_example
C compiler: /mingw64/bin/gcc
compiler target: x86_64-w64-mingw32
cmake -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=[/mingw64/bin/gcc]
-- Building for: Ninja
-- The C compiler identification is GNU 16.1.0
CMake Error at CMakeLists.txt:2 (project):
  The CMAKE_C_COMPILER:
    D:/a/_temp/msys64/mingw64/bin/gcc
  is not a full path to an existing compiler tool.
#+end_example

The message is hard to read because cmake *identified the compiler
successfully two lines earlier*. It found it; it cannot name the file.

Found on GitHub's =windows-2025= runner on 2026-08-23, the first cold
Windows build this project has ever done (job 97270577419).

* Cause — the =.exe= that MSYS2 supplies and Windows does not

MSYS2's POSIX layer opens =gcc= when only =gcc.exe= exists. So
=command -v gcc= answers =/mingw64/bin/gcc=, =[ -x ]= on it is true, and
=$compiler_path -dumpmachine= runs: *every check in build-libredwg.sh
passes*. cmake is a NATIVE program and does no such fallback — it converts
the argument to =<msys-root>/mingw64/bin/gcc=, tests =EXISTS=, and finds
nothing, because the file is =gcc.exe=.

This is the *same trap* that once shipped the Windows release binaries
without their extension (windows-release-binaries-lose-exe.issue): a name
MSYS2 resolves and Windows does not. It is worth naming as a class rather
than a coincidence — under MSYS2, a path that a shell test accepts is not
therefore a path a native tool accepts.

* Why nobody had seen it

The GitLab Windows runner cleans the workspace with =git clean -ffd=, not
=-ffdx= (deliberately: =-ffdx= dies outright when a CAD process holds a file
under =dist/= open — see .windows-clean in .gitlab-ci.yml). Ignored and
untracked files therefore survive between pipelines, including
=third-party/libredwg/build/= with its configured =CMakeCache.txt=. cmake is
never asked to configure again, so it is never asked the question that fails.

A hosted runner starts from nothing every time, which is how this surfaced.
It would equally have surfaced on the day someone cleared that build
directory on the Windows PC.

* Fix

=clautolisp/drawing-dwg/build-libredwg.sh=: after resolving the compiler and
normalising it to POSIX form, append =.exe= when the resolved path has no
extension and =$compiler_path.exe= exists. Guarded on =$host_uname= being
MINGW/MSYS/CYGWIN, and a no-op wherever the resolved path already exists as
named, so no other platform and no working setup is affected.

* Note

The POSIX normalisation immediately above the fix carries a long comment
about a drive letter's colon reaching cmake. That analysis stands (and
already records that cmake does *not* split =-D= on =:=, which the original
ticket wrongly claimed). The two are independent: one strips a prefix cmake
cannot use, the other supplies a suffix Windows requires.
